@using Servyx.Web.Authentication

@*
    Renders nothing and navigates to the sign-in page.

    Used as AuthorizeRouteView's NotAuthorized content, which is what handles a page that carries an explicit
    [Authorize] whose policy the current user fails. AuthenticationBoundary has already refused an
    unauthenticated caller before AuthorizeRouteView is reached, so this only fires for a caller who IS
    authenticated but fails a page's own policy (e.g. Servyx.Role.Admin on the Users page) — and only for that
    failure reached through an already-open interactive circuit (an in-app navigation to the page). The first,
    raw HTTP request to such a page never reaches this component at all: ASP.NET Core's own authorization
    middleware sees the same [Authorize] as endpoint metadata and issues a Forbid before any component
    renders, which the cookie handler turns into a redirect to OperatorAuthentication.HomePath instead (see
    its own remarks for why that path, specifically, and not this one).

    NavigateTo here carries no returnUrl, unlike that HTTP-level redirect — deliberately: GetLoginAsync bounces
    an already-authenticated caller straight back to whatever returnUrl it's given, so if this ever forwarded
    the refused page's own path, an authenticated-but-insufficiently-privileged caller reaching this component
    would ping-pong between it and /login forever, the same loop AccessDeniedPath's own remarks describe.
    Landing on / (via /login's no-returnUrl-means-home default) is what both paths end up doing today, so a
    caller sees the same outcome either way, just by two different routes.
*@

@code {
    [Inject]
    private NavigationManager Nav { get; set; } = default!;

    protected override void OnInitialized()
        => Nav.NavigateTo(OperatorAuthentication.LoginPath, forceLoad: true);
}
